@article{ WOS:000382228300098,
Author = {Lakshminarayana, G. and Kaky, Kawa M. and Baki, S. O. and Ye, Song and
   Lira, A. and Kityk, I. V. and Mahdi, M. A.},
Title = {Concentration dependent structural, thermal, and optical features of
   Pr3+-doped multicomponent tellurite glasses},
Journal = {JOURNAL OF ALLOYS AND COMPOUNDS},
Year = {2016},
Volume = {686},
Pages = {769-784},
Month = {NOV 25},
Abstract = {Tuning the structural, thermal, and optical properties of low phonon
   energy glasses such as tellurite glasses (phonon energy similar to 750
   cm(-1)) with suitable rare earth dopants is a key issue in the
   fabrication of solid state lasers and optical amplifiers. In this work,
   (70-x) TeO2-10 WO3-10 ZnO-5 TiO2-5 Na2O-(x) Pr2O3 (x = 1.0-5.0 mol \%)
   glasses were synthesized with high optical quality and characterized
   using Xray diffraction (XRD), Scanning electron microscopy and Energy
   dispersive X-ray analysis (SEM-EDAX), Attenuated total
   reflectance-Fourier transform infrared (ATR-FTIR), Raman spectroscopy,
   Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), Differential scanning calorimetry
   (DSC), optical absorption and luminescence techniques. The XRD and SEM
   measurements reveal the amorphous nature of all the prepared glasses and
   EDAX confirms all the elements present in the respective glasses. The
   presence of various functional groups such as stretching vibrations of
   Te-O bonds in the {[}TeO4] trigonal bi-pyramid units, symmetrical
   stretching or bending vibrations of Te-O-Te or O-Te-O linkages at corner
   sharing sites along the chains of TeO4, TeO3 and TeO3+1, stretching
   vibrations of W-O- and W=O bonds in WO4 tetragonal or WO6 octagonal
   units, vibrations of Zn-O bonds from ZnO4 groups, including
   nonhygroscopic nature of the glasses are confirmed by ATR-FTIR and Raman
   spectra, respectively. For Pr3+-doped glasses, from the DSC profiles the
   glass transition temperature (T-g), onset crystallization temperature
   (T-x), crystallization temperature (T-c), and melting temperature (T-m)
   are identified and the evaluated thermal stability values varied in the
   temperature range of 169-220 degrees C with increasing Pr3+ doping
   concentration. Further, the Pr3+-doped tellurite glasses demonstrate
   excellent glass stability with higher criterion of Hruby's value (H-R)
   between 1.9 and 3.9. From the measured optical absorption spectrum,
   experimental oscillator strengths are calculated and used to evaluate
   three phenomenological Judd-Ofelt (J-O) intensity parameters
   Omega(lambda)(lambda = 2, 4 and 6) and respective radiative properties
   such as radiative transition probabilities (A(R)), the branching ratios
   (beta(R)), and the radiative lifetime (tau(R)) of metastable states for
   1.0 mol \% Pr3+-doped glass. Five main emission transitions at P-3(0) ->
   H-3(5) (530 nm; green) with a shoulder at 543 nm, a weak band at D-1(2)
   -> H-3(4) (592 nm; orange), P-3(0) -> H-3(6) (615 nm; orange), P-3(0) ->
   F-3(2) (649 nm; red), and P-3(0) -> F-3(3) (686 nm; red) upon exciting
   at 486 nm (H-3(4) -> P-3(0)) wavelength are observed from the
   luminescence spectra of Pr3+-doped tellurite glasses. Following the
   energy level diagram, Pr3+ ion concentration quenching on the
   luminescence intensity has been explained by a nonradiative energy
   transfer between the ions through cross-relaxation and energy migration
   processes.
   The concentration dependent structural, thermal, and optical behaviors
   of Pr3+-doped tellurite glasses are understood and our systematic
   analysis could contribute towards the development of suitable optical
   devices fabrication. (C) 2016 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.},
